AArch64 arm64-userword-lostpublish-main
(* The dangerous direction of the user-word handoff, under upstream's protocol.

   arm64-userword-handoff-main asks whether the arriving owner can READ a stale
   in-flight address. That direction is benign: it makes GetMinUserWord's fold
   too LOW, so SafeTailAddress lags and iterators stall. Conservative.

   This asks the opposite, and it is not benign. The departing owner's
   EndInflightEnqueue write of the InflightInactive sentinel races the arriving
   owner's BeginInflightEnqueue write of its own live address -- a write-write
   race on the same user word, exactly analogous to the threadId race in
   arm64-release-plainstore. If the sentinel lands LAST, the arriving producer's
   in-flight publish is erased while it is still in flight. GetMinUserWord then
   folds a neutral value where a live one belongs, SafeTailAddress advances past
   a slot whose payload has not been written, and an iterator reads it.

   That is a torn read, not a stall.

   uw=9 is InflightInactive, uw=8 is the arriving owner's live publish. The
   condition is the arriving owner having claimed the slot and its publish
   having been overwritten by the departing owner's sentinel. *)
{ 0:X0=uw; 0:X1=tid; 0:X2=lce; 1:X0=tid; 1:X1=uw; uw=7; tid=3; lce=5; }
 P0            | P1               ;
 MOV X3,#9     | MOV X2,#0        ;
 STR X3,[X0]   | MOV X3,#1        ;
 STR XZR,[X2]  | CASAL X2,X3,[X0] ;
 STR XZR,[X1]  | MOV X4,#8        ;
               | STR X4,[X1]      ;
exists (1:X2=0 /\ uw=9)
